# docker run flags for the assessment-agent image, and the single source of truth
# for them (the Dockerfile's RUN note and .github/workflows/sandbox.yml read this).
# One flag per line. Consumers strip '#' lines and blank lines, so don't put
# comments at the end of a flag line. Paths are relative to the repo root.
# Why each flag is needed: deploy/entrypoint.sh.
# Never add --init (compose: init: true): the image runs its own tini as PID 1.
#
# Private cgroup namespace: the entrypoint remounts it rw and hands it to the worker.
# It refuses a host cgroupns, which would be the host's own hierarchy.
--cgroupns=private
# These caps are for the root entrypoint only. It drops every one of them, bounding
# set included, before exec'ing CMD. SYS_ADMIN: remount /sys/fs/cgroup rw.
# CHOWN: delegate it. SETUID/SETGID: become the worker. SETPCAP: drop the bounding
# set (setpriv silently skips that without it).
--cap-drop=ALL
--cap-add=SYS_ADMIN
--cap-add=CHOWN
--cap-add=SETUID
--cap-add=SETGID
--cap-add=SETPCAP
# Nothing regains privileges through a setuid or file-capability binary.
--security-opt=no-new-privileges
# Docker's /proc masks make the kernel refuse nsjail's procfs mount in its user
# namespace. Unmasking is safe only because nothing the server spawns holds a cap (a
# `docker exec` shell bypasses the entrypoint and does, but docker access is root).
--security-opt=systempaths=unconfined
# Grants user namespaces on Ubuntu >= 23.10. Load it on the host first:
# sudo apparmor_parser -r deploy/apparmor/assess-nsjail
# Where `sysctl -n kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns` is absent or 0, skip
# that and append --security-opt=apparmor=unconfined after these flags (last one wins).
--security-opt=apparmor=assess-nsjail
# Docker's default minus the SYS_ADMIN catch-all, plus what nsjail needs, native ABI
# only. Generated by scripts/gen-seccomp.py.
--security-opt=seccomp=deploy/seccomp.json
